ing without  you sort of pointing them to  uh to the different cues that would say  oh yeah no  now that you've explained it to me this  makes sense this does sound interesting  so once you've identified something you  want to apply for and um  and you've thought about you know the  motivation of the the agency and  providing the funding um there's some  other things you can do uh first of all  has anybody else in your department  applied for this award in the past it  may be that your registrar has access to  old applications uh it may be that some  of the students who have replied are  still here you can talk to them about  their experience and whether or not they  received any feedback and  have any insights into what works or  what doesn't work with an application  maybe some of your faculty have served  as reviewers for this organization and  they certainly that would be a great  resource to tell you what what the  reviewers are looking for  uh if you're applying for international  travel the macmillan center is a great  resource they've got knowledge experts  there who can talk to you about you know  what's going on in individual countries  and things that you might need to think  about  uh  you know  and problems that you might need to  address in the application pitfalls or  problems that you might need to overcome  uh that they  can say that a you know an agency might  want to see that you're knowledgeable  about  the uh the graduate writing lab uh i  want to say it's just it's an  outstanding resource i have not sent  anybody there who's ever come back and  told me that it was a waste of their  time i've had a lot of people come back  and say that they were really very  helpful  um they do a number of different things  including peer review writing groups  one-on-one mentoring with tutors  um  they're a fantastic resource if you're  applying for an award where the people  who are reviewing it are not necessarily  going to be  squarely in your field  becaus