 for a fifth or sixth year  student who's pretty far along in their  research already has drawn their  conclusions just needs to go out and  you know get some supporting data or you  know fill in some gap somewhere and  it's a much more specific you know um  you know detailed uh grants than they  might otherwise be applying for  that makes sense i have two more  questions for you they're both pretty  practical um the first person uh  um asks basically how do i know what a  reasonable budget looks like that isn't  quote  padded i'm not asking to spend the night  in a five-star hotel but do i have to  say that i will sleep in a one-star  hotel  um  you know the best way to answer this i'd  say is that  other people have probably gone to  wherever it is you're going and had to  provide a budget to and so it's a good  idea to just talk to other people who  have traveled uh and see what kinds of  budgets they have proposed there's some  resources online  um some of the funding agencies for  example will have guidelines as to what  they will uh provide for this or that of  the other thing and and  um  and if you have you know if you say you  know i'm going to go to to this country  and and yes the program will provide up  to you know 500 a month for for um you  know accommodations but i actually know  somebody in the country and and you know  i won't need that you know i i can pay  them you know  100 for expenses and you know the agency  will appreciate the fact that you're  finding ways to save money um  but i would say you know in terms of  knowing what to do just talk to other  people who have applied um i think the  macmillan center is probably a great  resource for for you know travel abroad  uh where they can say well this is what  sort of living expenses are in in the  country  um you know your travel expenses you  know go online and look at a variety of  airlines and figure out you know what  round trip tickets are gonna cost and  you know try to  pick the cheapest one that doesn't  involve you you know flying in a cargo  plane with crates full of chickens  um you know you don't have to cut it to  the to the bone and have like the  cheapest budget you can possibly find  but um but they do expect you to cut  costs where you can and um  you know but usually there are resources  online and certainly resources at the  university can say yes you know  living expenses in this country you know  for a traveler would be between  300 and 700 a month  for example and so you know  probably best then not to come down at  the 700 side you know you don't have to  say 300 you want to give yourself a  little room maybe but try to come in  under 500  but other people have done this before  you and have had experience and and you  know had budgets approved or had budgets  cut uh and so there should be resources  out there to help you figure out what's  appropriate  great thank you and the last question  the answer may be the same which is that  there are other people who can help you  with this but maybe you have particular  thoughts about who  um the question is really about cvs and  the person says i'm not sure if my cv is  properly formatted or if it has the  right information on it  do you have any suggestions about how  one should get feedback on their cv to  make sure that it's professionally ready  um i first of all i will just say that  that  you know there's a lot of different cvs  out there um there's there's different  styles it's not necessarily one right  way to do it um and i you know i myself  you know when i was younger and applying  for jobs depending on the nature of the  job i was applying for would move things  around on my cv um which would either  you know uh  which would emphasize my research or  would emphasize you know my teaching  experience or what have you depending on  the job i was applying for so it's not a  one size fits all um but i think that  the um uh office for career strategies  is a great resource for um  you know if you contact them and say you  need some help you need somebody to look  at your cv um and give you some feedback  on that they have people there that can  help you with that  great thank you so much uh dean harper  mangles that's not the most  you know extravagant way to end this  session right i think that was rather  kind of mundane but i think it really  important advice because i think it's  one of the many things that so many  applicants worry about when they're  making these applications and they're as  you well know  many people are doing these applications  while they're very busy with other work  and so trying to figure out ways to uh  to explain  what what people ought to be doing is  and how is just so key and so you know  thank you so much for for making such a  compelling argument for the importance  of of making application making these  applications i think you're absolutely  right that this is that applying in  these ways  really is key for people to learn how to  promote their own work and that funding  begets funding as as you say and that  you know for those reasons and others um  encouraging people  in you know the greater ritm network and  elsewhere to to be looking for these  opportunities i think is incredibly  beneficial even as it can be  hard and difficult in the course of  one's you know regular very busy life as  well so and i'll say you know it's it's  the mundane aspects of the application  that often people find daunting you know  they know what they're doing they  understand their research but then they  find out they have to pull in all these  other  pieces of information and and that gets  a lot of people caught up i i remember  you know the first time um  when i worked at northwestern before i  came to yale and my director came to me  and he said we're going to apply for an  institutional training grant now these  are large multi-million dollar rewards  that fund multiple faculty and multiple  students the the proposal itself the  sort of the scientific component to it  is only 10 to 15 pages  the entire proposal is about 200 pages  because suddenly you need  data about students going back 10 years  and how many people came in and where  did they wind up going and what's your  training program look like um and that  was really hard to do the first time  um but i got to tell you by the time we  did the fifth one we had a process we  had a lot of the data already and so  once we had the intellectual component  put together then we could just slot in  all those mundane elements and it got a  lot easier to do so this is one of the  things where i say it gets easier to  write these things as you go forward  because as you get used to doing the  mundane parts of it you can focus on the  interesting intellectual component  and then just add in all that mundane  stuff that needs to be part of the  application and it just gets so much  easier over time  well i really appreciate that and i  think this is the kind of training that  we get too little of um at least on our  side of the university so thank you so  much for your time today and for for  giving us the benefit of your skills i'm  sure people will follow up with you and  i know you've already shared your your  email address with people  um and so i would just add my voice to  encourage people to write you directly  if there's anything that they think you  might be able to help them with or  direct them elsewhere you i think  you've offered yourself up as a resource  so we really appreciate that yeah please  take advantage of me and this is why i'm  here so so you know if you have  questions please get in touch  all right thank you rob thank you  everybody my pleasure take care
 

